{"success": true, "name": "arc-compact-panel-encoding", "file": "references/session-f0caf098-demo-learning.md", "content": "# Session learning: lattice-like compact outputs

The test was a 24x28 repeating 3x3 panel field separated by color-9 bands, with distinctive colored values at selected separator intersections. Two demos confirmed that this general puzzle class can produce compact outputs, but neither established a test mapping: demo 1 was 23x29 -> 5x4, demo 2 was 26x29 -> 3x4, while the test was 24x28. Their compact outputs preserve some colors from the separator markings and include the filler/background value (9 in demo 1, 7 in demo 2). Dimensions do not match a simple count of all intersections or panels. The tested agent requested a third demo without stating a concrete pair of competing mappings or the predicted observation that would distinguish them; no verified output rule was obtained.

Reusable diagnostic: before requesting another example, lay out candidate coordinate maps and output conventions. For each demo, record exact positions/values of exceptional separator cells and compact output. Test possible selection of intersections, row/column reduction, edge inclusion, ordering, and background/default encoding against every observed pair. Make a further request only if the competing hypotheses yield a specific different predicted outcome, then immediately use the returned example to resolve that difference. Do not infer the test result from visual lattice resemblance or output shape alone.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-ws0/frozen/seen/play/users/u094/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/arc-compact-panel-encoding/references/session-f0caf098-demo-learning.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-demo-ledger", "file": "references/task-f0caf098-intersection-ledger.md", "content": "# Divider-crossing demos with varying compact outputs

This task family presented large regular lattices whose outputs were much smaller, with marked values placed on divider intersections. Across five examples, input/output dimensions varied substantially (23×29→5×4, 26×29→3×4, 22×30→1×1, 28×23→3×3, 29×14→5×4). The outputs echoed some values found at marked crossings, but the examples did not establish a direct full crossing-matrix extraction or a unique rule for edge inclusion, ordering, or default values. The 24×28 test's lattice appearance therefore did not justify a specific compact output.

Reusable response: maintain an explicit coordinate ledger of divider lines, exceptional intersections, and every output cell; compare candidate direct, cropped/selected, and panel-summary mappings against all demos. Before another request, state two competing mappings and a decision-relevant predicted observation. If no concrete discriminator exists, do not chain requests or submit a guess based only on visual resemblance. This is unresolved evidence, not a solved transformation.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-ws0/frozen/seen/play/users/u094/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/arc-demo-ledger/references/task-f0caf098-intersection-ledger.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-demo-ledger", "file": "references/session-f0caf098-output-representation.md", "content": "# Compact output representation and task-f0caf098 review

The demos repeatedly presented large regular lattices but returned compact outputs whose dimensions varied substantially. A key caution is that identifying separator crossings or extracting marker colors is only a candidate representation: neither the test's lattice dimensions nor a plausible crossing matrix establishes the output shape/content. Across demonstrations, explicitly align region/intersection positions to each compact output and test the precise encoding (including blank/default cells, omitted borders, ordering, and exceptional marker values). Require exact replay of every demo before submission.

In the reviewed instance, I made successive unsupported compact-grid guesses (including 4x4 and 5x5 crossing matrices) despite failed feedback. After any incorrect compact-output submission, restart representation inference from the demos and record which assumption each failure rejects; do not repeat the same candidate or cosmetically alter its entries. The available skill references already flag this output-dimension/mapping issue; this note captures the concrete failure pattern and emphasizes full representation validation before transfer.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-ws0/frozen/seen/play/users/u094/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/arc-demo-ledger/references/session-f0caf098-output-representation.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-demo-ledger", "file": "references/periodic-field-inference.md", "content": "# Periodic-field hypothesis from heterogeneous demos

When a demo appears unrelated to another by palette, dimensions, or local geometry, first inspect whether each input nevertheless encodes an ordered sequence or lattice that is expanded into a full periodic output. For a periodic candidate, independently derive the period, symbol/color order, direction, and phase/origin from the input markers and output. Verify the complete generated grid against every demo, including row/column transitions; a visually plausible periodic fill is not evidence by itself. Heterogeneity alone is not grounds either to dismiss a shared abstract rule or to repeatedly request demonstrations without a discriminating question.

This is a diagnostic technique, not a solved task-specific mapping. In the reviewed stream, the demos included sparse color tracks and an already periodic output; the test likewise contained diagonal color tracks, but no transfer rule was rigorously established before the next demo request. Future analyses should extract and validate the proposed sequence-to-field mapping before submission.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-ws0/frozen/seen/play/users/u094/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/arc-demo-ledger/references/periodic-field-inference.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-demo-delta-discipline", "file": "references/compact-region-mapping.md", "content": "# Compact region-to-output mapping

When a partitioned input has a much smaller output, investigate a region summary as a live task class instead of assuming a same-size edit or stamp. Identify separator rows/columns, compute the number and arrangement of regions, and associate each region with the corresponding output cell. Inspect marker presence, marker color/identity, count, and position within each region as candidate encodings. Verify the exact output—including default values for empty regions—against every demonstration before transfer. Output dimensions alone do not establish the mapping. Before spending another demo request, list at least two candidate encodings and state what observation in the next pair would distinguish them.

In a demo with sparse markers embedded in a regular partitioned canvas, the compact output preserved a 6×3 region arrangement and represented localized marker colors at corresponding output positions; most empty positions retained the canvas color. Treat this as a diagnostic pattern, not a universal rule: confirm separators and correspondence in the current task.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-ws0/frozen/seen/play/users/u094/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/arc-demo-delta-discipline/references/compact-region-mapping.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-demo-ledger", "file": "references/abstract-rule-vs-demo-heterogeneity.md", "content": "# Distinguish visual heterogeneity from task-rule drift

A fresh stream used demos with very different dimensions, palettes, backgrounds, and motifs from the test. The demos' observed edits were themselves disparate localized edits: demo 1 changed a pair of 5s to 6s; demo 2 changed a single 8 to 9; demo 3 changed a 7-cell to 9; demo 4 changed a local 7 region to 8; demo 5 changed two 7s to 3; demo 6 recolored a compact 2x3 patch from 2 to 5. No shared selector or abstract transformation was derived, and repeated requests were made without analyzing a cumulative delta ledger. The session's final candidate merely recolored the test's 8 structures to 4 without support from the demos.

Reusable inference lesson: neither assume that different-looking demos are unrelated nor force a common rule based on colors/salience. Precisely record each pair's delta, object context, and unchanged candidates; then seek measurable invariant relations (shape, relative position, local neighborhood, object role) across examples. Test any proposed rule by exact full-output replay on every demo. If deltas remain unlinked, further demos require a specific competing-hypothesis prediction; serial requests do not resolve uncertainty by themselves. For the test, keep its actual colors and structures unchanged unless a validated rule predicts edits.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-ws0/frozen/seen/play/users/u094/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/arc-demo-ledger/references/abstract-rule-vs-demo-heterogeneity.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-compact-panel-encoding", "file": "references/failed-output-grid-recovery.md", "content": "# Compact-output recovery after rejection

This task demonstrated a costly failure pattern: after a compact panel task's answer was rejected, subsequent answers repeated the same sparse marker interpretation, including duplicate grids, while one attempt accidentally had an invalid row width. Treat each negative result as falsifying the whole candidate rule. Log candidate grids/hypotheses, then derive the compact output dimensions and cell mapping from demos before submitting again. Validate dimensions and row widths separately from the transformation. Do not repeat a candidate or vary only its serialization; a new submission needs a materially different mapping that replays every available demo.
